  "id": "Compound::DB00690 - Gene::2555",
  "verbalization": "Flurazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabra2, a subunit of the Gaba-a receptor, through multiple paths with high confidence scores. These paths indicate a direct interaction between Flurazepam and Gabra2, which is involved in Gaba-a receptor activity. The shared involvement of Gabra2 in Gaba-a receptor activity across different binding sites (Gabra2, Gabrg1, and Gabrd) suggests that Flurazepam may modulate Gaba-a receptor function through a common mechanism. The presence of multiple binding sites for Flurazepam on different Gabra subunits implies that it may influence receptor activity through a general interaction with the Gaba-a receptor complex. The integrated evidence supports a role for Flurazepam in modulating Gaba-a receptor activity, potentially through binding to multiple Gabra subunits, which could lead to altered neurotransmitter signaling. This mechanism is consistent with the known pharmacological effects of benzodiazepines on GABAergic systems.",
